From 8998c9832e735b6ce8706fa63ac3049c71d3f110 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anurodh Pokharel <anurodhp@icloud.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 08:47:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] libdyld: don't self-register the dlopen-lock atfork triple
 (DAR-191)

_dyld_initializer() calls pthread_atfork(&beforeForkPrepareDlopen,
&afterForkParentDlopen, &afterForkChildDlopen) unconditionally at
libSystem init time -- real, unmodified upstream dyld source, exercised
in every process that loads libdyld.dylib.

On this port, the child-side handler (afterForkChildDlopen ->
_dyld_after_fork_dlopen_child() -> RuntimeState::
resetDlopenLockInForkChild(), third_party/dyld/dyld/DyldRuntimeState.cpp)
never returns for any forked child. Confirmed via layered instrumentation
(full trail in DAR-191): every one of launchd's 3 daemon children hung
at 100% host CPU immediately after job_start()'s fork(), before even
reaching bootstrap_init(). A marker inside libSystem_atfork_child()
(iokit project's fork_compat.c) showed all 3 children entering, and
never returning from, _pthread_atfork_child_handlers() -- the walk over
pthread_atfork()'s own registered-handler array. A marker inside that
walk (third_party/libpthread/src/pthread_atfork.c) showed a real,
non-corrupt single entry (atfork_count == 1) whose three function
pointers were address-matched byte-for-byte against libdyld.dylib's own
symbol table for beforeForkPrepareDlopen/afterForkParentDlopen/
afterForkChildDlopen (consistent 0x2c/0x58-byte spacing at every
observed slide) -- i.e. genuinely this registration, not a corrupted
pointer or some other caller. A further marker bracketing
afterForkChildDlopen()'s own call confirmed the hang is inside
_dyld_after_fork_dlopen_child(), whose only real body is guarded by
`libSystemHelpers->version() >= 2` -- checked directly
(third_party/dyld/libdyld/LibSystemHelpers.cpp), version() unconditionally
returns 2 on this port, so that specific "version() mismatch" theory
(already flagged as unproven in this port's own d13/DAR-8 writeup for
the OTHER, direct dyld atfork trio) is falsified for this lock. The
remaining candidate is libplatform's own
os_unfair_recursive_lock_unlock_forked_child() (third_party/libplatform/
src/os/lock.c), whose only non-trivial statement __LIBPLATFORM_CLIENT_
CRASH__s with "Lock was not held" if the lock looks unheld in the child
-- and this port's abort()-family path is already documented
(docs/launchd-pid1-postmortem.md Bug D) to spin forever instead of
terminating, since kill(getpid(), SIGABRT) has no real signal delivery
here. That matches the observed symptom exactly: no panic, no corpse,
100% CPU, kernel-side PCs bouncing through _lck_mtx_lock/_hw_lock_lock
via _exception_deliver/_bsd_ast (captured by an earlier live QEMU
monitor probe) -- the signature of a thread whose SIGABRT never
actually terminates it.

This is a second, previously-unknown instance of the same hazard class
already documented and worked around for the DIRECT
_dyld_atfork_prepare/_parent/_dyld_fork_child trio (iokit project's
build_launchd_syscalls.sh, "DYLD -- REAL, BUILT, AND DELIBERATELY NOT
CALLED", docs/backlog.md d13) -- that earlier fix only covered the three
direct calls libSystem_atfork_child() makes and didn't know about this
SEPARATE, indirect registration path, so it kept firing regardless.

Not calling this registration is not a regression: nothing on this port
relies on the dlopen lock being reset after fork (no concurrent
dlopen-during-fork scenario exists in this port's own daemon-spawn
path), and every forked child that was permanently stuck before even
reaching bootstrap_init() is now unblocked.

Verified by real QEMU boot (iokit project, qemu/dar191_fix_verify_pdtrace.log):
all 3 of launchd's daemon children (com.apple.launchctl.System,
com.apple.syslogd, org.puredarwin.console-login) now print "child after
runtime_fork" and "child bootstrap_init" -- both previously unreachable
in every prior trace, where children hung inside fork() itself before
ever returning 0 to the caller. qemu/dar191_fix_verify1.log (no
tracing) confirms no panic/regression on a plain boot. Root-causing the
exact byte state of apiLock.oul_value at the moment of the crash check
(real dyld source, #if BUILDING_DYLD, a materially bigger change
surface) is left as a real, separate follow-up -- see DAR-191. Boot now
reaches a new, later, separate stall inside bootstrap_init()'s own MIG
call, tracked as its own follow-up, not fixed here.
---
 libdyld/libdyldGlue.cpp | 71 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 71 insertions(+)

diff --git a/libdyld/libdyldGlue.cpp b/libdyld/libdyldGlue.cpp
index 38f6c03..d4f5717 100644
--- a/libdyld/libdyldGlue.cpp
+++ b/libdyld/libdyldGlue.cpp
@@ -130,11 +130,82 @@ static void afterForkChildDlopen()
 // This is called during libSystem.dylib initialization.
 // It calls back into dyld and lets it know it can start using libSystem.dylib
 // functions which are wrapped in the LibSystemHelpers class.
+//
+// iokit project (DAR-191, 2026-09-01): the pthread_atfork() registration
+// below is REAL, UNMODIFIED upstream dyld source, and is DELIBERATELY NOT
+// CALLED on this port -- a second, previously-unknown instance of the
+// same hazard class already documented and worked around for the direct
+// _dyld_atfork_prepare/_parent/_dyld_fork_child trio (see
+// tools/userland_staging/build_launchd_syscalls.sh's fork_compat.c, "DYLD
+// -- REAL, BUILT, AND DELIBERATELY NOT CALLED", docs/backlog.md d13).
+// That earlier fix skipped the three DIRECT calls libSystem_atfork_child()
+// makes; it did not know about this SEPARATE, indirect registration path
+// -- every process that loads libdyld.dylib (i.e. everything on this
+// port) self-registers this exact prepare/parent/child triple via the
+// generic pthread_atfork() mechanism at libSystem init time, so it fires
+// through _pthread_atfork_child_handlers()'s array walk regardless of
+// what the direct six-call sequence in fork_compat.c does or doesn't call.
+//
+// Real, QEMU-proven symptom: every one of launchd's 3 daemon children
+// (com.apple.launchctl.System, com.apple.syslogd,
+// org.puredarwin.console-login) hung permanently, 100% host CPU, no
+// panic, no corpse, immediately after job_start()'s fork() -- boot never
+// reached pd_launchd_load_daemons_dir()'s own post-import work, let alone
+// exec. Root-caused via layered instrumentation (see DAR-191 in Linear
+// for the full trail): a raw write(2)-based marker bracketing every call
+// in libSystem_atfork_child() showed all 3 children reaching, and never
+// returning from, the LAST call, _pthread_atfork_child_handlers(); a
+// marker inside that function (third_party/libpthread/src/
+// pthread_atfork.c) showed a real, non-corrupt, single registered entry
+// (atfork_count == 1) whose prepare/parent/child function pointers were
+// address-matched byte-for-byte (after accounting for a consistent
+// 0x2c/0x58-byte spacing) against libdyld.dylib's own real symbol table
+// for beforeForkPrepareDlopen/afterForkParentDlopen/afterForkChildDlopen
+// -- i.e. this exact registration, not a corrupted pointer or some other
+// caller. A further marker bracketing afterForkChildDlopen()'s own call
+// into gDyld.apis->_dyld_after_fork_dlopen_child() confirmed the hang is
+// inside that call, whose only real body (RuntimeState::
+// resetDlopenLockInForkChild(), third_party/dyld/dyld/DyldRuntimeState.cpp)
+// is guarded by `libSystemHelpers->version() >= 2` -- version() was
+// checked directly (third_party/dyld/libdyld/LibSystemHelpers.cpp) and
+// unconditionally returns 2 on this port, so the "version() mismatch"
+// theory the earlier d13 writeup flagged as unproven is FALSIFIED for
+// this specific lock. The remaining, unfalsified mechanism: libplatform's
+// os_unfair_recursive_lock_unlock_forked_child() (third_party/libplatform/
+// src/os/lock.c) is the only statement in that whole call chain capable
+// of not returning normally -- it __LIBPLATFORM_CLIENT_CRASH__s with
+// "Lock was not held" if the lock's oul_value looks unheld in the child,
+// and on this port __LIBPLATFORM_CLIENT_CRASH__'s abort()-family path is
+// already documented (docs/launchd-pid1-postmortem.md Bug D) to spin
+// forever instead of terminating the process, because kill(getpid(),
+// SIGABRT) has no real signal delivery here -- an exact match for the
+// observed 100%-CPU-no-corpse symptom and for the kernel-side lock-spin
+// PCs (_lck_mtx_lock/_hw_lock_lock via _exception_deliver/_bsd_ast) an
+// earlier live QEMU-monitor probe captured. Root-causing the exact byte
+// state of apiLock.oul_value at the moment of that check (i.e. exactly
+// why it looks unheld) needs deeper instrumentation inside
+// RuntimeState::takeDlopenLockBeforeFork()/resetDlopenLockInForkChild()
+// themselves -- real dyld source (#if BUILDING_DYLD), a substantially
+// bigger change surface than this ticket's fix -- and is left as the
+// real, still-open, precisely-scoped follow-up (see docs/backlog.md).
+//
+// Not calling this registration is not a regression from the pre-DAR-191
+// state: nothing on this port relies on the dlopen lock being reset
+// after fork (there is no concurrent dlopen-from-another-thread-during-
+// fork scenario in this port's own daemon-spawn path), and every forked
+// child is unblocked by this change where it was previously permanently
+// stuck before even reaching exec.
 void _dyld_initializer()
 {
     gDyld.apis->_libdyld_initialize(&sHelpers);
 
+#if 0 /* DAR-191: deliberately not called, see comment above */
     pthread_atfork(&beforeForkPrepareDlopen, &afterForkParentDlopen, &afterForkChildDlopen);
+#else
+    (void)beforeForkPrepareDlopen;
+    (void)afterForkParentDlopen;
+    (void)afterForkChildDlopen;
+#endif
 }
 
 
-- 
2.50.1 (Apple Git-155)

